⚠️ **IMPORTANT: Ctrl+C does not actually stop the scan — the file is still read to EOF**

Setting `tally.partial = true` and returning skips the *tallying*, but `SessionLineScanner.scan` (session_line_scanner.dart:100) has no early-stop: it keeps issuing `readRange` chunks until `cursor == size`. On the exact multi-GB surface this PR targets, "cancel" still costs the full sequential read of the remaining file (seconds of GB-scale I/O) before the PARTIAL report and exit 130 are produced — and `isCanceled` is never polled again once `canceled` is true, so even the line-level early-out degrades to pure wasted I/O.

The PR description promises "Ctrl+C cancels into a PARTIAL report with exit 130"; today it eventually produces a partial report, but the cancellation is not timely. Suggested fixes, cheapest first:

```dart
// Option A: abort via exception, caught around scan.
class _InfoScanCancel implements Exception {}

// in the callback:
if (canceled) {
  tally.partial = true;
  throw const _InfoScanCancel();
}

// around the scan:
try {
  await SessionLineScanner(...).scan(...);
} on _InfoScanCancel {
  // partial tally stands
}
```

- Option B: extend `SessionLineScanner.scan` with an optional `bool Function()` stop predicate checked per chunk — other long scans (repair, search) would benefit too.

Either keeps the honest PARTIAL semantics while making the cancel real; add a test that a cancel mid-file on a multi-chunk fixture performs fewer reads than the full size.

